# The minimum number of usable patterns the privacy list must carry.
#
# WHY THIS NUMBER LIVES HERE, IN THE PUBLIC REPO, AND NOT IN THE PRIVATE LAYER
#
# `privacy-check` refuses an EMPTY pattern list (exit 2) and accepts a SHORTENED one (exit 0). The
# identity that can shorten it is the implementer — which is the identity the list constrains, and
# which holds `write` on `quince-local` by ruling (quince#44, 2026-07-27), because the layer is a
# living document an agent must be able to maintain without the Operator as a hop.
#
# The control that resolves this everywhere else in the project is a reviewed PR against a
# protected branch. It is NOT AVAILABLE on `quince-local`: branch protection there returns
# "Upgrade to GitHub Pro or make this repository public to enable this feature", because the repo
# is private and protection on private repositories needs a paid plan. Measured, not assumed.
#
# So the floor lives in `quince`, which IS protected: one approval, required checks, no force
# pushes, admins included. Lowering this number is therefore a reviewed change in a protected repo,
# by an approver who is not the author — which restores the review model to the one artifact that
# could not have it, without moving the list itself.
#
# WHERE IT ACTUALLY BITES, because an earlier version of this line said "on every box and in CI"
# and the second half is false. Measured: `gates` runs `privacy-check-test`, `forge-watch-test` and
# `preflight-test`, all of which build SYNTHETIC layers by design — that is what makes them runnable
# anywhere — and the `privacy-check` target that reads the real list is standalone rather than part
# of the ladder. Nothing in .github/workflows/ci.yml references the private layer at all.
#
# **CI never sees the real pattern list, so nothing there can detect a trim.** The floor bites ON
# THE BOXES: the commit-time sweep a session runs before every push, and `preflight`, which
# delegates its verdict to `privacy-check` against the real layer at service start. That is a real
# control and not an always-on independent one, and the difference is the whole of what a reader
# needs when weighing the residual risk on quince#44 — whose ruling says detection is all that
# remains once prevention is unavailable and narrowing is ruled out.
#
# A count, deliberately, not a checksum: the list is expected to CHANGE, and a checksum would fail
# on every legitimate edit until someone updated it, which trains people to update it without
# looking — the exact reflex this project keeps filing issues about.
#
# WHAT THIS FLOOR COVERS, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT. It covers SHRINK: patterns removed, so the gate
# sweeps for less. It does NOT cover SUBSTITUTION — a same-count rewrite is invisible to it, and
# measured to be so:
#
#   3 real patterns,  floor 3 → a planted match exits 1 (caught)
#   3 junk patterns,  floor 3 → the SAME planted match exits 0 (clean, and the protection is gone)
#
# Same count, floor satisfied, gate green. The identity that can trim the list can equally rewrite
# it, and the rewrite is the quieter of the two. So this closes one of two weakening modes, and
# anyone weighing the residual risk on quince#44 should read it as exactly that rather than as
# "the list is protected".
#
# A count cannot close substitution; only known-positive probes can, by proving the matcher still
# MATCHES rather than merely compiling. `privacy-check` supports a canary for that and it IS
# populated on the boxes: every run reports `canary ok — the matcher matches known-positive input
# (10 probe(s))`. So the matcher is proven rather than merely compiled, and a rewrite that broke
# matching outright would be caught.
#
# THAT NARROWS SUBSTITUTION; IT DOES NOT CLOSE IT, and the reason is unchanged by the canary
# existing. The probes must match the private patterns, so they are sensitive themselves and live
# in the private layer — writable by the same identity that can rewrite the list, the same problem
# one level down. A substitution that rewrites the probes in step still goes green. The canary
# makes that a two-file act instead of a one-file act, which is a real cost to an attacker and not
# a control. Recorded on quince#44's residual-risk register, not solved here.
#
# RAISE THIS when the list grows, in the same PR that grows it if the two are ever in one place.
# A floor left below the real count still blocks the shrink it was set for; it simply protects
# less than it could.
#
# This number is not sensitive. It says how many patterns exist, never what they are.
16
